 Invoices: usage is costed per request at list price, summed per model, converted to cents, then the key's discount is applied.
+
+## Rate limiting
+
+Every request except `GET /health` is rate limited per API key. The limiter runs before routing and authentication, so requests that end in 401, 403, 404 or any other error count too. The key is the `x-api-key` header as sent; requests without one (or with an empty one) share the key `anonymous`.
+
+Limits use fixed windows. A key's window starts at its first request and lasts `windowMs`. The first `limit` requests in the window are allowed and the rest are rejected. The first request at or after the end of the window starts a new window. Configure it with `createServer({ rateLimit: { limit, windowMs } })`; the default is `{ limit: 60, windowMs: 60000 }`, and a missing field takes its default. `limit` must be a positive integer and `windowMs` a positive number, or `createServer` throws a `RangeError`. All times come from the server's `now` clock.
+
+| Header | On | Value |
+|---|---|---|
+| `X-RateLimit-Limit` | every response except `GET /health` | `limit` |
+| `X-RateLimit-Remaining` | every response except `GET /health` | requests still allowed in the current window after this one; `0` when rejected |
+| `Retry-After` | 429 only | whole seconds until the window ends, rounded up, at least 1 |
+
+A rejected request never reaches routing. It gets status 429 and the body `{"error": "rate_limited", "retryAfter": <seconds>}`, where `retryAfter` matches `Retry-After`.
+
+`GET /health` is never rejected, does not count toward any key, and has no rate-limit headers.
+
+Counts are kept in memory: they reset when the server restarts and are not shared between server processes. Keys are not checked against known keys, so the limit is per value of `x-api-key`.
